conductor -- the conductor of a finite ring map
***********************************************

  * Usage: 
        conductor F
        conductor R
  * Inputs:
      * F, a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,, $R \rightarrow S$, a
        finite map with $R$ an affine reduced ring
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, an affine domain.  In this case,
        $F : R \rightarrow S$ is the inclusion map of $R$ into the integral
        closure $S$
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, of $R$ consisting of all $d \in
        R$ such that $dS \subset F(R)$

Description
===========

Suppose that the ring map $F : R \rightarrow S$ is finite: i.e. $S$ is a
finitely generated $R$-module.  The conductor of $F$ is defined to be $\{ g \in
R \mid g S \subset F(R) \}$. One way to think about this is that the conductor
is the set of universal denominators of S over R, or as the largest ideal of R
which is also an ideal in S. An important case is the conductor of the map from
a ring to its integral closure.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y,z]/ideal(x^7-z^7-y^2*z^5);                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : icFractions R                                                           |
|                                                                             |
|       3   2                                                                 |
|      x   x                                                                  |
|o2 = {--, --, x, y, z}                                                       |
|       2   z                                                                 |
|      z                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : List                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : F = icMap R                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|                                                    QQ[w   , w   , x..z]     |
|                                                        5,0   4,0            |
|o3 = map (-------------------------------------------------------------------|
|                    2                          2     2       2     3   2     |
|          (w   z - x , w   z - w   x, w   x - w   , w   x - y z - z , w   w  |
|            4,0         5,0     4,0    5,0     4,0   5,0               5,0 4,|
|                                                                             |
|                                                       QQ[w   , w   , x..z]  |
|                                                           5,0   4,0         |
|o3 : RingMap ----------------------------------------------------------------|
|                       2                          2     2       2     3   2  |
|             (w   z - x , w   z - w   x, w   x - w   , w   x - y z - z , w   |
|               4,0         5,0     4,0    5,0     4,0   5,0               5,0|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|-------------------------------------, R, {x, y, z})                         |
|       2      2   3          2    2                                          |
|  - x*y  - x*z , w    - w   y  - x z)                                        |
|0                 5,0    4,0                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|---------------------------------------- <-- R                               |
|          2      2   3          2    2                                       |
|w    - x*y  - x*z , w    - w   y  - x z)                                     |
| 4,0                 5,0    4,0                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : conductor F                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|             4     3   2 2   4    5                                          |
|o4 = ideal (z , x*z , x z , x z, x )                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

If an affine domain (a ring finitely generated over a field) is given as input,
then the conductor of $R$ in its integral closure is returned.

+------------------------------------+
|i5 : conductor R                    |
|                                    |
|             4     3   2 2   4    5 |
|o5 = ideal (z , x*z , x z , x z, x )|
|                                    |
|o5 : Ideal of R                     |
+------------------------------------+



If the map is not icFractions(R), then *note pushForward:
(Macaulay2Doc)pushForward_lpRingMap_cmModule_rp, is called to compute the
conductor.

Caveat
======

Currently this function only works if F comes from a integral closure
computation, or is homogeneous

See also
========

  * *note integralClosure: integralClosure, -- integral closure of an ideal or
    a domain
  * *note icFractions: icFractions, -- fractions integral over an affine domain
  * *note icMap: icMap, -- natural map from an affine domain into its integral
    closure
  * *note pushForward: (Macaulay2Doc)pushForward_lpRingMap_cmModule_rp, --
    compute the pushforward of a module along a ring map

Ways to use conductor:
======================

  * "conductor(Ring)"
  * "conductor(RingMap)"

