idealizer -- compute Hom(I,I) as a quotient ring
************************************************

  * Usage: 
        (F,G) = idealizer(I,f)
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, whose endomorphism ring we'll
        compute
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, a nonzerodivisor
        in $I$
      * Variable, a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, 
      * Index, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Strategy => a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value {},
        possible elements include ``Vasconcelos''
      * Verbosity => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 0,
        larger numbers give more information
      * *note Index: idealizer_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmIndex=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value 0, Sets the starting index on the new variables used to
        build the endomorphism ring Hom(J,J)
      * *note Variable: idealizer_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariable=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value "w", Sets the name of the indexed variables introduced in
        computing the endomorphism ring Hom(J,J).
  * Outputs:
      * F, a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,, The inclusion map from $R$
        into $S = Hom_R(I,I)$
      * G, a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,, $frac S \rightarrow frac
        R$, giving the fractions corresponding to each generator of $S$.

Description
===========

The idealizer of $I$, computed as target F, is the largest subring of the
fraction field of ring I in which $I$ is still an ideal.  Note that this is NOT
the common use of the term in commutative algebra.

This is a key subroutine used in the computation of integral closures.

+------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y]/(y^3-x^7)                |
|                                          |
|o1 = R                                    |
|                                          |
|o1 : QuotientRing                         |
+------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(x^2,y^2)                   |
|                                          |
|             2   2                        |
|o2 = ideal (x , y )                       |
|                                          |
|o2 : Ideal of R                           |
+------------------------------------------+
|i3 : (F,G) = idealizer(I,x^2);            |
+------------------------------------------+
|i4 : target F                             |
|                                          |
|                 QQ[w   , x..y]           |
|                     0,0                  |
|o4 = -------------------------------------|
|           2    2   2      3            5 |
|     (w   x  - y , w    - x y, w   y - x )|
|       0,0          0,0         0,0       |
|                                          |
|o4 : QuotientRing                         |
+------------------------------------------+
|i5 : first entries G.matrix               |
|                                          |
|       2                                  |
|      y                                   |
|o5 = {--, x, y}                           |
|       2                                  |
|      x                                   |
|                                          |
|o5 : List                                 |
+------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note ringFromFractions: ringFromFractions, -- find presentation for f.g.
    ring
  * *note integralClosure: integralClosure, -- integral closure of an ideal or
    a domain

Ways to use idealizer:
======================

  * "idealizer(Ideal,RingElement)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note idealizer: idealizer, is a *note method function with options:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

