integralClosure(Ring) -- compute the integral closure (normalization) of an affine domain
*****************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note integralClosure: integralClosure,
  * Usage: 
        R' = integralClosure R
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, a quotient of a polynomial ring
        over a field
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Keep: integralClosure_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmKeep=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => a
        *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value null, of variables of R
      * *note Limit: integralClosure_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => an
        *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value infinity, do a partial
        integral closure
      * *note Variable: integralClosure_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariable=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value "w", set the
        base letter for the indexed variables introduced while computing the
        integral closure
      * *note Verbosity: integralClosure_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerbosity=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 0, display a
        certain amount of detail about the computation
      * *note Strategy: integralClosure_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value {}, of some of the
        symbols: AllCodimensions, Radical, RadicalCodim1, Vasconcelos,
        StartWithOneminor, SimplifyFractions
  * Outputs:
      * R', a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, the integral closure of R
  * Consequences:
      * The inclusion map $R \rightarrow R'$ can be obtained with *note icMap:
        icMap,.
      * The fractions corresponding to the variables of the ring R' can be
        found with *note icFractions: icFractions,

Description
===========

The integral closure of a domain is the subring of the fraction field
consisting of all fractions integral over the domain.  For example,

+----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y,z]/ideal(x^6-z^6-y^2*z^4-z^3);|
+----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : R' = integralClosure R                   |
|                                              |
|o2 = R'                                       |
|                                              |
|o2 : QuotientRing                             |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : gens R'                                  |
|                                              |
|o3 = {w   , x, y, z}                          |
|       3,0                                    |
|                                              |
|o3 : List                                     |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : icFractions R                            |
|                                              |
|       2                                      |
|      x                                       |
|o4 = {--, x, y, z}                            |
|       z                                      |
|                                              |
|o4 : List                                     |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : icMap R                                  |
|                                              |
|o5 = map (R', R, {x, y, z})                   |
|                                              |
|o5 : RingMap R' <-- R                         |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i6 : I = trim ideal R'                        |
|                                              |
|                     2   3      2     3       |
|o6 = ideal (w   z - x , w    - y z - z  - 1)  |
|             3,0         3,0                  |
|                                              |
|o6 : Ideal of QQ[w   , x..z]                  |
|                  3,0                         |
+----------------------------------------------+

Sometimes using *note trim: (Macaulay2Doc)trim, provides a cleaner set of
generators.

If $R$ is not a domain, first decompose it, and collect all of the integral
closures.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : S = ZZ/101[a..d]/ideal(a*(b-c),c*(b-d),b*(c-d));                      |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : C = decompose ideal S                                                 |
|                                                                           |
|o8 = {ideal (c, b), ideal (c - d, b - d), ideal (d, c, a), ideal (d, b, a)}|
|                                                                           |
|o8 : List                                                                  |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : Rs = apply(C, I -> (ring I)/I);                                       |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : Rs/integralClosure                                                   |
|                                                                           |
|        ZZ            ZZ           ZZ         ZZ                           |
|       ---[a..d]     ---[a..d]    ---[a..d]  ---[a..d]                     |
|       101           101          101        101                           |
|o10 = {---------, --------------, ---------, ---------}                    |
|         (c, b)   (c - d, b - d)  (d, c, a)  (d, b, a)                     |
|                                                                           |
|o10 : List                                                                 |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : oo/prune                                                             |
|                                                                           |
|        ZZ         ZZ         ZZ      ZZ                                   |
|o11 = {---[a, d], ---[a, d], ---[b], ---[c]}                               |
|       101        101        101     101                                   |
|                                                                           |
|o11 : List                                                                 |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+

This function is roughly based on Theo De Jong's paper, An Algorithm for
Computing the Integral Closure, J. Symbolic Computation, (1998) 26, 273-277.
This algorithm is similar to the round two algorithm of Zassenhaus in algebraic
number theory.

There are several optional parameters which allows the user to control the way
the integral closure is computed.  These options may change in the future.

Caveat
======

This function requires that the degree of the field extension (over a pure
transcendental subfield) be greater than the characteristic of the base field.
If not, use *note icFracP: icFracP,. This function requires that the ring be
finitely generated over a ring.  If not (e.g. if it is f.g. over the integers),
then the result is integral, but not necessarily the entire integral closure.
Finally, if the ring is not a domain, then the answers will often be incorrect,
or an obscure error will occur.

See also
========

  * *note icMap: icMap, -- natural map from an affine domain into its integral
    closure
  * *note icFractions: icFractions, -- fractions integral over an affine domain
  * *note conductor: conductor, -- the conductor of a finite ring map
  * *note icFracP: icFracP, -- compute the integral closure in prime
    characteristic

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note integralClosure(Ring): integralClosure_lpRing_rp, -- compute the
    integral closure (normalization) of an affine domain
